## Problem Statement

`ti list` has two filters for exploring Dependencies between tickets, `--depends X` and `--blocked-by X`. Their names mislead users:

- `--blocked-by X` returns the tickets that **block** X (X's blockers), the exact opposite of what the name reads as ("tickets blocked by X").
- `--depends X` is ambiguous: it could mean "tickets that depend on X" or "tickets X depends on". (It actually means the former.)
- The two flags *sound* like synonyms but return opposite ends of the relation.
- Neither name matches the vocabulary the rest of TicGit uses: the relation is created with `ti depends` and shown in `ti show` as "Depends" / "Blocks".

On top of that:

- Both filters silently hide blockers or dependents that are sub-issues (because `ti list` hides sub-issues by default). A user asking "what blocks X?" can get an empty list when an open sub-issue is in fact blocking X — a false "nothing is blocking" answer.
- Saved views store whatever ticket reference the user typed (often a short prefix), which can become ambiguous as tickets accumulate.
- The CLI integration test for `--blocked-by` is currently failing: it invokes a `ti blocked-by` subcommand that does not exist.

## Solution

Rename the filters so each one reads as a predicate on the listed tickets and mirrors the domain vocabulary (see CONTEXT.md: **Dependency**, **Depends on**, **Blocks**):

- `ti list --depends-on X` — tickets that depend on X (downstream: who waits on X).
- `ti list --blocks X` — tickets that block X (upstream: who X waits on).

The old `--depends` and `--blocked-by` flags are removed outright (no aliases). When either new flag is given, sub-issues are always included. The open-only default still applies, so by default only tickets that are *currently* blocking/waiting are shown; `--all` includes closed ones. Relations are direct only. The ticket argument is required. Saved views pin the ticket by its full id.

Given `ti depends B -t A` (A depends on B, B blocks A):

| Command | Result |
|---|---|
| `ti list --depends-on B` | A |
| `ti list --blocks A` | B |
| `ti list --depends-on A` | (nothing) |
| `ti list --blocks B` | (nothing) |

## User Stories

1. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X` to list the tickets that block X, so that I can see what I must finish before X can proceed.
2. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --depends-on X` to list the tickets that depend on X, so that I can see what finishing X will unblock.
3. As a TicGit user, I want the flag names to match the "Depends" and "Blocks" labels in `ti show`, so that I can predict a flag's result from what a ticket displays.
4. As a TicGit user, I want the flag names to match the `ti depends` command vocabulary, so that I only have to learn one word per direction.
5. As a TicGit user, I want `--depends-on X` to return exactly the tickets whose "Depends" list contains X, so that the filter behaves as a literal lookup.
6. As a TicGit user, I want `--blocks X` to return exactly the tickets whose "Blocks" list contains X, so that the filter behaves as a literal lookup.
7. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X` to include blockers that are sub-issues, so that I never wrongly conclude X is unblocked.
8. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --depends-on X` to include dependents that are sub-issues, so that I see every ticket X is holding up.
9. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X` to show only open blockers by default, so that I see only what is still actually blocking X (consistent with `ti next`).
10. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X --all` to include closed blockers, so that I can review the full history of X's dependencies.
11. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --depends-on X --all` to include closed dependents, so that I can see everything that ever waited on X.
12. As a TicGit user, I want the relationship filters to return only direct relations, so that results match the stored relation and `ti next`'s notion of "unblocked".
13. As a TicGit user, I want to pass X as a full id or a unique prefix, so that I can type short references like other commands allow.
14. As a TicGit user, I want a clear error when X does not resolve to a ticket (unknown or ambiguous prefix), so that I don't get a silently empty list.
15. As a TicGit user, I want `--depends-on` and `--blocks` to require a value, so that `ti list --blocks <view-name>` is never misread.
16. As a TicGit user, I want `--depends-on` and `--blocks` to combine with other filters (tag, assignee, state, search, order) using AND semantics, so that I can narrow results, e.g. "open bugs blocking X".
17. As a TicGit user, I want to combine `--depends-on X` and `--blocks Y` in one query, so that I can find tickets sitting between two others in a chain.
18. As a TicGit user who used the old flags, I want `--depends` and `--blocked-by` to fail with an "unexpected argument" error, so that I notice the rename instead of getting silently different results.
19. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --help` to describe each relationship flag in terms of Depends on / Blocks, so that the help text is unambiguous.
20. As a TicGit user, I want to save a view that includes `--blocks X` or `--depends-on X`, so that I can re-run a relationship query by name.
21. As a TicGit user, I want a saved view to pin the ticket by its full id, so that the view keeps pointing at the same ticket even after new tickets make my typed prefix ambiguous.
22. As a TicGit user, I want `ti views list` to describe relationship filters using the new flag names, so that the description matches what I would type.
23. As a TicGit user, I want `--json` and `--markdown` output to work unchanged with the new filters, so that scripts and agents can consume results.
24. As an AI agent using TicGit, I want unambiguous relationship flags, so that I can reliably determine which tickets block the one I'm working on before starting.
25. As a maintainer, I want the CLI test suite to pass, so that the broken `ti blocked-by` test no longer masks regressions.
26. As a maintainer, I want test fixtures named after their role (blocker, dependent), so that tests document the relation direction correctly.

## Implementation Decisions

- **Vocabulary**: Follow CONTEXT.md. A **Dependency** is one directed relation read two ways: "A depends on B" ≡ "B blocks A". "Blocked by" is an avoided term and must not appear in flags, fields or help text.
- **CLI (`ti list`)**: Replace the `--depends` flag with `--depends-on <TICKET>` and the `--blocked-by` flag with `--blocks <TICKET>`. Both take a required value (id or unique prefix), resolved through the store's existing id resolution. No aliases for the old names; clap's default "unexpected argument" error is the migration signal.
- **Filter struct (query module)**: Rename the fields `depends` → `depends_on` and `blocked_by` → `blocks` (both `Option<Uuid>`). Semantics unchanged: `depends_on: Some(X)` keeps tickets whose depends-on set contains X; `blocks: Some(X)` keeps tickets whose blocks set contains X. Direct relations only — no transitive walk.
- **Sub-issue bypass**: In the filter application, when `depends_on` or `blocks` is set, the hide-sub-issues rule is not applied. The status/state defaults (open-only unless `--all`, `--status` or `--state`) are unchanged and still apply.
- **Saved views / last filters**: Rename the saved-view fields `depends` → `depends_on` and `blocked_by` → `blocks`. Store the ticket's full UUID (resolved at `ti list` time, before the last filters are recorded) instead of the raw string the user typed. Old keys (`depends`, `blocked_by`) are not migrated; they are ignored on load. Loading a saved view resolves the stored full id as usual.
- **View description**: The saved-view description shows `--depends-on <id>` / `--blocks <id>`. It may shorten the UUID for display.
- **TUI**: Only mechanical updates for the renamed fields (the TUI keeps passing "none" for both). No new TUI capability.
- **Help text**: `--depends-on`: "Show tickets that depend on this ticket." `--blocks`: "Show tickets that block this ticket."
- **Rename only**: No change to how Dependencies are stored or created (`ti depends`), and no change to `ti show` rendering.

## Testing Decisions

- Good tests assert externally observable behaviour: which ticket ids come back from `ti list --json` for a given set of Dependencies and flags. They do not inspect state files or internal structs beyond the public `Filter` / `apply` interface.
- **Primary seam — CLI integration tests** (existing `TestRepo` harness, prior art: the existing `list_depends_filters_dependent_tickets` and saved-view tests in the CLI test suite). Cover:
  - `--depends-on B` returns A; `--blocks A` returns B; the reversed queries return nothing.
  - A sub-issue blocker is returned by `--blocks X` without `--subissues`; likewise for `--depends-on`.
  - A closed blocker is hidden by default and shown with `--all`.
  - `--depends` and `--blocked-by` fail with a non-zero exit.
  - Unknown ticket reference fails with a non-zero exit.
  - Save/replay: `ti list --blocks <prefix>` → `ti views save v` → `ti list v` returns the same tickets; `ti views list` shows `--blocks` with the full id.
  - Replace the broken `list_blocked_by_filters_blocked_tickets` test (which calls a non-existent `ti blocked-by` subcommand) with a correct `--blocks` test that creates the Dependency via `ti depends`.
- **Secondary seam — `query::apply` unit tests** (prior art: existing filter tests in the query module, e.g. `filter_by_dependency`). Cover the literal field lookup for both filters and the sub-issue bypass. Rename the misleading fixture: the ticket inserted with a `blocks` entry is the **blocker**, not "blocked".
- No new seams introduced.

## Out of Scope

- Transitive / recursive dependency queries (e.g. "everything upstream of X"). Better served by a future tree or graph view; to be filed as a separate ticket.
- A CLI-wide "current ticket" sentinel (e.g. `--blocks .`) or defaulting the value to the checked-out ticket. To be filed as a separate ticket.
- Exposing relationship filters in the TUI.
- Backward-compatibility aliases or migration of old saved-view keys.
- Changes to how Dependencies are created, stored, or rendered.
- Repeatable flags (`--blocks X --blocks Y`).

## Further Notes

- The original commits (a35c101, 84c2d7c) are on master but in no tagged release, which is why the old flags are removed without deprecation.
- The existing filter logic is already correct for the new names: current `--depends` logic becomes `--depends-on`; current `--blocked-by` logic becomes `--blocks`.
- Domain terms are recorded in CONTEXT.md (Dependency, Depends on, Blocks).